 A non-integer `<id>` for `assign` or `close` is rejected with a usage error on stderr and
 exit code 2. No event is appended.
+
+### CLI-010: OVERDUE marker in list
+
+`ticketdesk list` prints the word `OVERDUE` on the line of each ticket that is overdue per
+TKT-014. The word sits between the state and the title. The line is
+`#<id> [<priority>] <state> OVERDUE <title>`, with the state padded to 8 characters as in
+CLI-006. The marker is plain uppercase text with no color.
+
+The line of a ticket that is not overdue is exactly the line of CLI-006. The order of the
+lines does not change, see TKT-013. In this example ticket 1 is overdue, ticket 2 is not,
+and ticket 3 is closed:
+
+```
+#1 [P1] open     OVERDUE Printer on fire
+#2 [P3] assigned Paper jam
+#3 [P2] closed   Password reset
+```
+
+The goals and non-goals of the feature are in
+[Overdue tickets](tickets.md#overdue-tickets).
+
+TODO: the request says overdue tickets "show as OVERDUE". This text assumes that the word
+joins the state and does not replace it. An agent then still sees whether an overdue
+ticket has an assignee. The cost is that a title that starts with `OVERDUE ` reads like
+the marker. The alternative is to print `OVERDUE` in place of the state. Confirm with the
+user.
+
+### CLI-011: overdue
+
+`ticketdesk overdue` prints one line per overdue ticket, in the order of TKT-017. Each
+line is the line that `list` prints for that ticket, see CLI-010. It prints no other
+ticket, no header, and no count. It appends no event, see LOG-001.
+
+With no overdue ticket, `overdue` prints nothing and exits with code 0. That covers a log
+with no tickets and a log file that does not exist, see LOG-006.
+
+A corrupt log stops `overdue` as it stops every command, see LOG-007. How the CLI reports
+that failure is the open TODO under CLI-007. This item does not settle it.
+
+TODO: two assumptions need the user. The first is that the lines show no due time and no
+amount of lateness. The second is exit code 0 when no ticket is overdue. A non-zero code
+would let a script branch on "nothing overdue". Confirm both with the user.
+
+### CLI-012: overdue takes no arguments
+
+`ticketdesk overdue` takes no ticket ID, no filter, and no other value. A value or an
+unknown option after `overdue`, as in `ticketdesk overdue 3`, prints usage to stderr and
+exits with code 2. It prints nothing to stdout.
