+
+---
+
+## Overdue tickets
+
+Support agents see which tickets missed their due time, and can list only those tickets.
+The list of overdue tickets starts with the ticket that has been overdue longest. Before
+this feature, the due time of TKT-005 exists and no command shows it. An agent cannot tell
+a late ticket from a ticket that is on time.
+
+Status on 2026-11-05: proposed, not built, and not signed off by the user. The behavior is
+in TKT-014 to TKT-017 below. The command surface is in CLI-001, CLI-006, and CLI-010 to
+CLI-012 in [`cli.md`](cli.md). Each `TODO:` paragraph under those items is an open question.
+It names the assumption that the text makes until the user answers.
+
+Goals:
+
+1. An agent who reads `ticketdesk list` sees which tickets are overdue, with no due time
+   to work out. Covered by TKT-014, TKT-015, and CLI-010.
+2. An agent gets only the overdue tickets from one command, the longest overdue first.
+   Covered by TKT-017, CLI-011, and CLI-012.
+3. Everything else stays as it is. A ticket that is not overdue prints as it does today,
+   and no log changes. Covered by TKT-016 and CLI-010.
+
+Non-goals:
+
+- No fourth state. The states of TKT-001 and the forward-only rule do not change.
+- No lateness history. A ticket closed after its due time is not reported as late
+  anywhere.
+- No change to due times. The hours of TKT-005 stay. There are no business hours, no
+  paused clock, and no due time set by hand.
+- No alerts. An agent learns that a ticket is overdue only from `list` or `overdue`.
+- No "due soon" warning. A ticket is overdue or it is not.
+- No due time and no amount of lateness in any output.
+- No filter and no option on `overdue`, such as by assignee or by priority.
+- No change to the order of `list`, or to the output of `create`, `assign`, and `close`.
+
+### TKT-014: When a ticket is overdue
+
+A ticket is overdue when it is `open` or `assigned` and the current time is later than its
+due time. The due time is the one of TKT-005. At the exact due time the ticket is not
+overdue yet. The current time is the moment the command runs, on the same clock that
+stamps events, see LOG-004.
+
+A `P1` ticket created 4 hours and 1 minute ago is overdue. A `P1` ticket created 3 hours
+and 59 minutes ago is not.
+
+TODO: the request says tickets that "pass their due time". This text reads that as
+strictly later than the due time. Confirm with the user.
+
+### TKT-015: A closed ticket is never overdue
+
+A `closed` ticket is not overdue, whenever it was closed. Closing an overdue ticket ends
+its overdue condition at once. A ticket closed after its due time is not overdue either.
+
+TODO: this assumes that overdue means "needs action now". The other reading is a report of
+every ticket that missed its due time, closed ones included. Confirm with the user.
+
+### TKT-016: Overdue changes nothing else
+
+A ticket that becomes overdue keeps its state, its assignee, and its due time. No event is
+appended when the due time passes, so the log file stays byte for byte the same. Assigning
+and closing an overdue ticket follow TKT-006 to TKT-011 as for any other ticket. Assigning
+an overdue ticket leaves it overdue.
+
+TODO: this assumes that overdue is a marker and not a fourth state. A fourth state needs
+changes to TKT-001, LOG-002, LOG-003, and INV-001. Confirm with the user.
+
+### TKT-017: Overdue listing order
+
+The overdue listing returns every overdue ticket and no other ticket. It sorts them by due
+time ascending, so the ticket that has been overdue longest comes first. Tickets with the
+same due time are sorted by ID ascending.
+
+The order follows the due time. It does not follow the ID, the creation time, or the
+priority.
+
+| ID | Priority | Created | Due | Position in the overdue listing |
+|----|----------|---------|-----|---------------------------------|
+| 1 | `P3` | 74 hours ago | 2 hours ago | 2 |
+| 2 | `P2` | 30 hours ago | 6 hours ago | 1 |
+| 3 | `P1` | 5 hours ago | 1 hour ago | 3 |
+
+TODO: the request gives no order for tickets with the same due time. ID ascending is an
+assumption taken from TKT-013. Confirm with the user.
